[Music]  what does 87 cents have to do with a  multi-million euro election campaign  what are companies like these doing at  political party events  and how come this neo-nazi party is  getting funding from the german state  it's all to do with the way political  parties are financed in germany  the general principles of  this party regulation and financial  regulation i think they're fair or at  least they're well intended but we have  seen in the past that there have been  many loopholes and that the system could  be manipulated  in germany there's no limit on how much  parties can spend on election  campaigning  but some parties have more to spend than  others  here's how the disparity looked at the  last federal election in 2017.  you can clearly see the two biggest  parties the christian democrats and the  social democrats spent at least triple  the amount of the smaller parties  to fully understand this let's go back  to the coins because this is about  counting every cent  these are the key sources of funding for  german political parties membership fees  fees paid by mps  donations other sources like sponsoring  assets shares publications and events  and then there's the big one  state funding  this system is supposed to make party  financing fair transparent and give  parties equal opportunity  and this is how it works  the more votes a party gets the more  public money it gets  public support is measured by three  things votes membership fees and  donations  for every voter party gets it also gets  87 cents from the state  for the first 4 million votes the figure  is even a little more 1 euro 6 cents per  vote  then for every euro a party gets in  membership fees it also gets 45 cents  from the state  this 45 cents per euro rule also applies  for personal donations  donations from people not the ones from  companies  but there are regulations  you can only get these 45 cents for  donations up to 3 300 euros per person  per  year and there's a limit for the total  amount of state financing  in 2021 this limit was set at 200  million 49  468 euros  smaller parties benefit from this system  too  they might have fewer supporters but  they still collect votes membership fees  and donations and according to what  we've just learned this means they get  money from the state  here's an example  last year the neo-nazi party npd got 370  632 euros 85 cents from the german state  it's a very small amount compared to  what the larger parties are getting but  a lot of germans aren't happy about it  because the npd has been ruled as  anti-constitutional  there is a push to stop public money  going to the npd  the government has asked the country's  top court to stop state funding flowing  to the extreme right  but the court doesn't seem to be in a  big hurry that was in 2019 and we're  still waiting for the verdict  that's one problem in the system but  critics highlight other loopholes  there's a lack of transparency there is  no  limit  to financial influence for example so  donations to political parties so  there's there's no legal maximum  indeed in germany there's no limit on  the amount parties can accept in  donations  to understand it better let's have a  look at the details there are different  transparency rules for small  medium and large donations  donations under 500 euros can stay  anonymous  if donations are more than 10 000 euros  parties have to publicly declare where  they come from they do this in their  annual reports  the problem here however is that the  reports usually appear with a very long  delay of one to two years  only donations over 50 000 euros have to  be declared immediately  another loophole in the system has to do  with these guys here and plenty of other  industry players and business  representatives  instead of making large donations big  companies often sponsor party events  instead  for example a car manufacturer  displaying products at a party  conference where they can also network  with politicians  sponsoring doesn't have to be declared  in the party's financial reports  and this is uh  it's really annoying and from my  perspective that sponsoring activity is  still  buried in this income from other  activities category in the annual  reports of parties and that means it is  invisible  put differently in transparent  that brings us to another issue the  authority that's supposed to check if  parties are sticking to the rules and  find them if not  critics say that body is not fit for  purpose  for example it cannot proactively  investigate parties  and it's also under the control of the  bundestag president who belongs to a  political party himself in the current  government the bundestag president is a  member of angela merkel's christian  democrats  so there's potential for conflict of  interest  what we need is simply  ameliorating the system as it is  rendering the supervisory body more  independent from politics  and  recognizing that donations at least  above 10 000 euro can really make an  impact and so  rendering donner's identity  transparent immediately  a look at the details makes it clear  there are many loopholes in the system  and experts say addressing these would  make party financing fairer  but for this year's election it's too  late for any changes  right now what matters to parties and  their bank accounts is getting as many  votes as possible on september 26th  because the scents really do add up

